      "text": "To solve for \\(a\\) and \\(b\\), we will follow a step-by-step approach.\n\n1. **Finding the smallest positive two-digit multiple of \\(3\\):**\n\n   A two-digit number ranges from \\(10\\) to \\(99\\). We need to find the smallest number in this range that is a multiple of \\(3\\).\n   \n   The smallest two-digit number is \\(10\\). To check if \\(10\\) is a multiple of \\(3\\), we perform the division:\n   \\[\n   10 \\div 3 \\approx 3.33\n   \\]\n   Since \\(3.33\\) is not an integer, \\(10\\) is not a multiple of \\(3\\).\n\n   The next number to check is \\(11\\):\n   \\[\n   11 \\div 3 \\approx 3.67\n   \\]\n   \\(3.67\\) is not an integer, so \\(11\\) is not a multiple of \\(3\\).\n\n   The next number is \\(12\\):\n   \\[\n   12 \\div 3 = 4\n   \\]\n   Since \\(4\\) is an integer, \\(12\\) is a multiple of \\(3\\).\n\n   Therefore, the smallest positive two-digit multiple of \\(3\\) is:\n   \\[\n   a = 12\n   \\]\n\n2. **Finding the smallest positive three-digit multiple of \\(4\\):**\n\n   A three-digit number ranges from \\(100\\) to \\(999\\). We need to find the smallest number in this range that is a multiple of \\(4\\).\n   \n   The smallest three-digit number is \\(100\\). To check if \\(100\\) is a multiple of \\(4\\), we perform the division:\n   \\[\n   100 \\div 4 = 25\n   \\]\n   Since \\(25\\) is an integer, \\(100\\) is a multiple of \\(4\\).\n\n   Therefore, the smallest positive three-digit multiple of \\(4\\) is:\n   \\[\n   b = 100\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculating \\(a + b\\):**\n\n   Now that we have \\(a = 12\\) and \\(b = 100\\), we add these two values together:\n   \\[\n   a + b = 12 + 100 = 112\n   \\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{112}\n\\]",
